The multithreading sample's star sphere returns a vec3 from two functions
declared float - hash33 and starField - which today's glslang refuses and the
2016 glslang that made the shipped starsphere.frag.spv compiled into invalid
SPIR-V (spirv-val: "OpReturnValue Value's type does not match OpFunction's
return type"). NVIDIA's compiler (580.178.04) dereferences null on that module
in vkCreateGraphicsPipelines: the sample segfaulted natively, and took QEMU
down with it in the guest. Both now return what they are declared to; the
values are the ones the old module computed, since the vec3 was the scalar
three times over and only its first component was ever taken.

fetch_vulkan_samples.sh compiles the SPIR-V of every shader a patch touches
again, since the samples load the .spv and not the GLSL.

--- a/data/shaders/multithreading/starsphere.frag
+++ b/data/shaders/multithreading/starsphere.frag
@@ -15,10 +15,10 @@
 {
 	p3 = fract(p3 * HASHSCALE3);
     p3 += dot(p3, p3.yxz+19.19);
-    return fract(vec3((p3.x + p3.y)*p3.z + (p3.x+p3.z)*p3.y + (p3.y+p3.z)*p3.x));
+    return fract((p3.x + p3.y)*p3.z + (p3.x+p3.z)*p3.y + (p3.y+p3.z)*p3.x);
 }
 
